            <div xml:id="d721">

                <pb facs="0799" n="701" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg701"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1614: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Technical Board</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stevens</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-19T10:00:00+08:26"><hi rendition="#italic">July 19,
                                1920—10 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 20—6:34
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Your cable July 15, 6 p.m.<anchor n="58" xml:id="d721-f1"/> I would not take any
                    steps that would endanger the success of negotiations but if the idea is to
                    continue railway agreement in its present form I would have nothing <pb
                        facs="0800" n="702" xml:id="pg702"/> to do with it as it is a farce which
                    has not been respected by anybody. Whoever undertakes to run railway must be
                    given full authority which can be enforced. I have had no support from the
                    military of any nation in the enforcement of any order or instructions; …
                    Besides the matter of absolute control, provision must be made for finances to
                    be placed in presenting [<hi rendition="#italic">under international?</hi>]
                    control, for the Russians have not the slightest idea of economy. The Allied
                    Committee should be done away with; it has been of no positive service and in
                    some respects handicap; passing resolutions without any serious attempt or power
                    to make them effective gets nowhere in this country. Committee has no practical
                    knowledge of railways and their activities are about what might be expected. I
                    can easily understand why negotiations proceed slowly; a certain people playing
                    for time until they stack cards here when their real plans will become clear to
                    everyone. I will await further information from the Department before acting,
                    asking a reply as soon as possible.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stevens</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
